# Do Vietnamese people grow their good?
Yes, I would say many Vietnamese people still grow their own food, especially those living in rural areas. Since Vietnam has a long agricultural tradition, it's quite common for families to have small gardens where they grow vegetables, herbs, and fruit trees for daily consumption.

Even in urban areas, some people grow plants on their balconies, rooftops, or in small home gardens. Although they may not produce enough food to be completely self-sufficient, they enjoy having access to fresh produce and knowing exactly how it was grown.

Besides saving money, I think growing food also gives people a sense of satisfaction and helps them stay connected to nature. That's why this practice remains quite popular in Vietnam today.

# What kind of food is generally popular in your country?
I would say traditional Vietnamese food is by far the most popular in my country. People here enjoy a wide variety of traditional dishes, ranging from noodle-based meals like pho and bun bo Hue to rice dishes and fresh spring rolls.

Street food is also an important part of Vietnamese food culture. It's affordable, convenient, and incredibly diverse. You can easily find dishes such as banh mi, grilled meat skewers, or sticky rice at roadside stalls and local markets.

Overall, Vietnamese people tend to prefer fresh, flavorful food that combines different ingredients and herbs, which is one of the reasons our cuisine is so well-known around the world.
